@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Stored Procedures Introduction";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Stored Procedures Introduction
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            A Stored Procedure is a precompiled collection of SQL statements
            stored inside SQL Server. It can be executed whenever required
            and is commonly used to perform database operations in a
            structured and reusable way.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS A STORED PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is a Stored Procedure?
        </h2>

        <p>
            A Stored Procedure is a named group of one or more SQL statements
            stored in the SQL Server database.
        </p>

        <p>
            Instead of writing the same SQL query repeatedly, we can create
            a stored procedure once and execute it whenever required.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Simple Definition
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">
                A Stored Procedure is a reusable set of SQL statements
                stored in SQL Server and executed using its procedure name.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHY USE STORED PROCEDURES
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Why Use Stored Procedures?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Stored Procedures provide several benefits when developing
            database applications.
        </p>

        <ul class="mb-4">

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Reusability:</strong>
                The same procedure can be executed multiple times.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Maintainability:</strong>
                Database logic can be maintained in one place.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Security:</strong>
                Users can be given permission to execute procedures
                without giving direct access to underlying tables.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Performance:</strong>
                SQL Server can optimize and reuse execution plans for
                stored procedures.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                <strong>Reduced Repetition:</strong>
                Common database operations do not need to be written again
                and again.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic Syntax of a Stored Procedure
        </h2>

        <p>
            The basic syntax for creating a stored procedure is:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE ProcedureName
AS
BEGIN

    -- SQL statements

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Simple Stored Procedure Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Consider an <strong>Employees</strong> table. We can create a
            stored procedure that retrieves all employees.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetEmployees
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT *
    FROM Employees;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            The procedure named <strong>GetEmployees</strong> contains a
            SELECT statement that returns all records from the
            <strong>Employees</strong> table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTING STORED PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Executing a Stored Procedure
        </h2>

        <p>
            After creating a stored procedure, we can execute it using
            the <code>EXEC</code> statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>EXEC GetEmployees;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-success">

            <strong>Output:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                The query returns all records from the
                <strong>Employees</strong> table.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         STORED PROCEDURE WITH SELECT
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Stored Procedure for a Specific Query
        </h2>

        <p>
            A stored procedure can contain any valid SQL statement.
            For example, we can create a procedure to retrieve employees
            from a specific department.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetITEmployees
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT *
    FROM Employees
    WHERE Department = 'IT';

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p>
            Execute the procedure using:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>EXEC GetITEmployees;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         STORED PROCEDURE COMPONENTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Components of a Stored Procedure
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Component</th>
                        <th>Description</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>CREATE PROCEDURE</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Used to create a new stored procedure.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Procedure Name</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            The unique name used to identify the procedure.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>Parameters</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Optional values passed to the procedure.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>AS</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Indicates the beginning of the procedure definition.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>BEGIN ... END</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Contains the SQL statements executed by the procedure.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <strong>EXEC</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Used to execute a stored procedure.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         ADVANTAGES
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Advantages of Stored Procedures
        </h2>

        <div class="alert alert-info">

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Reusable database logic.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Easier database maintenance.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Better security through controlled permissions.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Can accept input and output parameters.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Can contain multiple SQL statements.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Useful for implementing business logic at the database level.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Important Points
        </h2>

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    A stored procedure is stored inside the database.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    It can contain one or multiple SQL statements.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Parameters can be used to make procedures dynamic.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The <code>EXEC</code> command is commonly used to execute
                    a stored procedure.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Stored procedures can be created, modified and removed.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0 p-4">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                Stored Procedures are reusable collections of SQL statements
                stored inside SQL Server. They help organize database logic,
                improve maintainability and security, and can accept
                parameters to perform dynamic database operations.
                In the next topic, we will learn how to create a stored
                procedure in detail.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


